window.docContents[231].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Maximum___-Minimum___-Comparison___-and-Uniqueness-for-Harmonic-Functions":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Model Dirichlet Problems and Harmonic Rigidity","header":"5.3.4. Maximum, Minimum, Comparison, and Uniqueness for Harmonic Functions","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Model-Dirichlet-Problems-and-Harmonic-Rigidity--Maximum___-Minimum___-Comparison___-and-Uniqueness-for-Harmonic-Functions"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--Weak-and-Entropy-Solutions--Curved-shocks-with-varying-states":{"contents":"The jump calculation also works when the interface bends and the states on\neither side vary. Let the interface be  and define\n. Integrating by parts on the two sides\nleaves an interface contribution with coefficient .\nRankine–Hugoniot cancels this contribution; entropy requires the analogous\ncoefficient to have a sign.\n\nLet  and .\nAssume  solves  on  and \nsolves it on . Suppose at every interface pointand  is convex on the interval between these two traces. Then the\npiecewise field is a weak solution and satisfies every  convex\nentropy inequality on all of .\n\nThe classical residuals vanish in the two open regions. Integration by\nparts with the moving boundary reduces the weak residual to the\nRankine–Hugoniot coefficient, which is zero. For an entropy pair\n with , the same calculation givesThe integral on the right uses the time parameter along the interface.\nConvexity of the flux between the downward traces, together with convexity\nof the entropy, makes its coefficient nonnegative. Thus every nonnegative\ncompactly supported test gives the entropy inequality. Neither constant\ntraces nor a straight interface is used.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Symmetric Systems and Conservation Laws\u0009Conservation Laws\u0009Weak and Entropy Solutions","header":"7.5.2.6.1. Curved shocks with varying states","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Symmetric-Systems-and-Conservation-Laws--Conservation-Laws--Weak-and-Entropy-Solutions--Curved-shocks-with-varying-states"},"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution--The-kernel___s-derivative___-step-by-step":{"contents":"The kernel time derivative below uses positivity of  when\ndifferentiating its real power. The calculation applies to the Gaussian\nbranch at positive time.\n\nopen PDE.Common HeatEquation in\nexample {n : ℕ} (x : ℝ^n) (t : ℝ) (ht : t > 0) :\n    deriv (fun s ↦ fundamentalSolutionHeat (n := n) x s) t =\n      (‖x‖ ^ 2 / (4 * t ^ 2) - (n : ℝ) / (2 * t)) *\n        fundamentalSolutionHeat (n := n) x t := by\n  have hgt_pos : 0 < 4 * Real.pi * t := by positivity\n  -- Work with the `t > 0` branch of the definition via\n  -- an eventual equality near `t`.\n  have hbranch :\n      (fun s ↦ fundamentalSolutionHeat (n := n) x s) =ᶠ[nhds t]\n        (fun s ↦\n          (4 * Real.pi * s) ^ (-(n : ℝ) / 2) *\n            Real.exp (-(‖x‖ ^ 2 / (4 * s)))) := by\n    filter_upwards [eventually_gt_nhds ht] with s hs\n    simp [fundamentalSolutionHeat, not_le_of_gt hs, mul_comm]\n  rw [hbranch.deriv_eq, fundamentalSolutionHeat, ite_eq_right (not_le_of_gt ht)]\n  cas using hgt_pos\n\n\nThe squared heat-ball slice radius is\n. Its derivative calculation uses\n and  to differentiate the logarithm.\n\nopen HeatEquation in\nexample (n : ℕ) {r τ : ℝ} (hr : r ≠ 0) (hτ : τ ≠ 0) :\n    HasDerivAt (heatBallSliceRadiusSq n r)\n      (2 * (n : ℝ) * (Real.log (r ^ 2 / (4 * Real.pi * τ)) - 1)) τ := by\n  unfold heatBallSliceRadiusSq\n  cas\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Diffusion and weak evolution","header":"6.1.3. The kernel's derivative, step by step","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Diffusion-and-weak-evolution--The-kernel___s-derivative___-step-by-step"}});